## Overview ##
The goal of Part 2 was to enhance the backend by implementing secure user authentication and authorization using Google OAuth 2.0,
    Each diary entry must now be associated with an authenticated user, 
    only logged-in users can view, create, update, or delete their own entries.

This is a Session-based authentication,
    Easier testing with Postman, Session cookies are automatically managed by browsers,
    Once the React frontend is built in Part 3, authentication will switch to JWT.

Core authentication middleware framework was passport
    express-session does Server-side session management
    cors does Cross-origin resource sharing

## Google Cloud Console Workflow ##
OAuth 2.0 is an authorization framework that allows applications to obtain limited access to user accounts on an HTTP service.
    It works by delegating user authentication to the service that hosts the user account
    and authorizing third-party applications to access the user account.

Create a Google Cloud Project
    Configure OAuth Consent Screen, Go to APIs & Services 
    Fill in required fields, name, email
    Generate OAuth 2.0 Credentials
    Put Authorized redirect URIs :http://localhost:5000/auth/google/callback
    Add Credentials to .env File

## Authentication Flow Description ##
After successful Google authentication, server creates session with user ID 
    Session ID is stored in connect.sid
    Browser automatically sends cookie
    Server verifies HMAC and looks up session data
    session destroyed on logout

